dict(family='cx-content-podcast-show-notes', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['marketing-without-money#29'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Brief podcast show notes that rank and serve.','audience':'Content team.','constraints':'Must be more than a transcript dump.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — show notes that earn search traffic and serve listeners. Corpus: marketing-without-money#29 — the episode as a content asset. Brief: the notes are a standalone article — the episode's argument in text, with the key timestamps, the resources linked, and the quotable lines pulled — the guest's actual words from the transcript, never paraphrased into stronger claims; the transcript is attached, not featured (transcripts rank poorly and read worse); the title targets the search query the episode answers (the episode title serves subscribers; the page title serves search) — the title promises what the episode delivers, labeled by intent, never clickbait; one CTA (subscribe, or the episode's offer — not five). Rules: notes written by someone who listened (summary-by-transcript is detectable and thin); the guest's links and bio included (reciprocity earns the share). Voice: restrained — show notes fail from being an afterthought; this brief makes them a first-class asset. An episode without notes is a broadcast; with notes, it is a library.""",
 output="""# Brief: Podcast Show Notes\n**Outcome:** search traffic + listener service — a first-class asset, not an afterthought.\n**Structure:** the episode's argument as a standalone article → key timestamps → resources linked → quotable lines. Transcript attached, not featured.\n**SEO:** page title targets the query the episode answers (episode title serves subscribers; page title serves search).\n**Rules:** written by someone who listened (transcript-summaries are detectable); guest links + bio included (reciprocity earns the share); one CTA.\nAn episode without notes is a broadcast. With notes, it is a library.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-content-help-center-brief', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-bing-webmaster#2'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Brief help-center articles that deflect tickets.','audience':'Support-led content team.','constraints':'Written from actual tickets.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — fewer tickets, solved users. Corpus: 1s-bing-webmaster#2 — the content must match what exists (here: the product as it actually behaves). Brief: articles sourced from ticket themes (the top 20 ticket drivers, in the users' words — the titles match what users type); each article: the answer first (no throat-clearing), steps with screenshots of the current UI, the "if this did not work" path (the escalation, not a dead end); every article tested against the ticket it came from (would this have solved it?); stale articles killed on a schedule (screenshots of last year's UI are worse than no article). Rules: no marketing in help content (the help center is not a funnel); the search on the help center actually works (the best article is useless if unfindable). Voice: restrained — help content is judged by the ticket queue, not by pageviews. Deflection measured honestly: ticket theme volume before and after.""",
 output="""# Brief: Help-Center Articles\n**Outcome:** fewer tickets, solved users.\n**Sourcing:** top 20 ticket themes, titles in the users' words.\n**Structure:** answer first → steps with current-UI screenshots → "if this did not work" escalation path.\n**Rules:** tested against the originating ticket (would this have solved it?); screenshots kept current (stale UI is worse than none); no marketing in help content; help-center search must work.\n**Measured by:** ticket theme volume before/after — not pageviews. Help content is judged by the queue.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-content-social-proof-placement', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-growth-design#8'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Write the social-proof placement rules.','audience':'Design and copy teams.','constraints':'Proof must be specific.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — proof that answers hesitation. Corpus: 1s-growth-design#8 — proof at the hesitation point: the placement is the strategy. Rules: every proof element sits at the hesitation it answers (pricing → pricing page; "does it work for companies like us" → logo wall with segments, not a logo soup); proof is specific or it is decoration — numbers, names, contexts ("4.9/5 from 2,300 reviews" beats "loved by thousands"; "Acme cut close time 40%" beats "Acme loves us"); logos only with permission and only of recognizable relevance (50 unknown logos are wallpaper); the proof ladder — stats for the skimmers, quotes for the readers, case studies for the convinced; no proof above the hero's claim (proof supports the claim it follows — orphaned proof is decoration). Voice: opinionated — most social proof is wallpaper; this system makes it load-bearing. Every proof element must survive the "so what" test from a skeptic.""",
 output="""# Social-Proof Placement Rules\n**Outcome:** proof that answers hesitation — load-bearing, not wallpaper.\n**Placement:** each proof sits at the hesitation it answers. Logos segmented by relevance, not soup.\n**Specificity:** numbers, names, contexts — "4.9/5 from 2,300 reviews" beats "loved by thousands." Anonymous or vague proof is decoration.\n**Ladder:** stats (skimmers) → quotes (readers) → case studies (convinced). Proof follows the claim it supports — orphaned proof is wallpaper.\n**Test:** every element survives a skeptic's "so what." Wallpaper fails; evidence ships.""",
 rules=['no-invented-proof','honest-labeling']),
dict(family='cx-content-brand-voice-guide', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['marketing-without-money#21'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Write the brand voice guide.','audience':'Everyone who writes for the brand.','constraints':'Must be usable, not aspirational.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — writing that sounds like one company. Corpus: marketing-without-money#21 — the founder's voice as the brand's spine. The guide: the voice in three words (e.g., "direct, warm, specific" — each with a do/don't example); the register rules (contractions yes; jargon no; the reader addressed as "you"); the banned list (hype adjectives and corporate filler — with replacements); the proof habit (claims carry numbers and sources — the voice is honest, so the copy is); the edge (the one opinion the brand holds that competitors will not — the spine). Rules: the guide fits on one page (a 40-page voice guide is never read); every rule has an example (rules without examples are wishes); the guide is enforced in review (voice drift corrected, not tolerated). Voice of the guide itself: it demonstrates the voice it describes — direct, warm, specific. A voice guide that bores is a voice guide that failed.""",
 output="""# Brand Voice Guide (one page)\n**Voice in three words:** [direct, warm, specific] — each with a do/don't example.\n**Register:** contractions yes; jargon no; reader as "you."\n**Banned:** [hype adjectives, corporate filler…] — with replacements.\n**Proof habit:** claims carry numbers + sources. The voice is honest, so the copy is.\n**The edge:** the one opinion competitors will not hold — [stated].\n**Enforcement:** one page, every rule exemplified, drift corrected in review.\nThis guide demonstrates the voice it describes. A voice guide that bores has failed.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling']),
dict(family='cx-content-pricing-faq', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-baymard#2'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Brief the pricing FAQ.','audience':'Copywriter.','constraints':'Must answer the questions buyers actually fear.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — pricing hesitation, dissolved. Corpus: 1s-baymard#2 — cost transparency is the researched pattern; the FAQ is where the feared questions live. Brief: the questions buyers actually fear — "what does it cost when the trial ends," "can I cancel myself or must I call," "what happens to my data if I leave," "are there usage limits that trigger charges," "do you raise prices" — each answered plainly, with the true answer (the FAQ is not the place to discover the answers are bad — if an answer is embarrassing, fix the policy, not the copy); no invented questions (source: sales calls, support tickets, the pricing page's exit comments). Rules: every answer short enough to be believed, and every answer carries the true policy, labeled as the policy it is — the FAQ does not soften numbers with 'starting at' vagueness; the cancellation answer is the most important on the page (buyers decide on the exit before the entrance). Voice: direct — the pricing FAQ is a trust document; evasive answers are detected instantly and punished at checkout.""",
 output="""# Brief: Pricing FAQ\n**Outcome:** pricing hesitation, dissolved.\n**Questions (real fears only):** trial-end cost → self-serve cancellation? → data on exit → usage-charge triggers → price increases?\n**Standard:** plain true answers. If an answer embarrasses, fix the policy — not the copy. Every answer short enough to be believed.\n**Most important:** the cancellation answer — buyers decide on the exit before the entrance.\nSourced from sales calls, tickets, exit comments — never invented. Evasive pricing answers are detected instantly and punished at checkout.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-content-seasonal-campaign-brief', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['1s-tiktok-academy#5'], license_class='TIER-1S-SYNTH',
 brief={'objective':'Brief the holiday campaign content.','audience':'Creative team.','constraints':'Must not rely on discounts alone.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — holiday revenue without margin destruction. Corpus: 1s-tiktok-academy#5 — native creative; the seasonal brief protects the brand while selling. Brief: the angle (the gift story, the year-in-review, the limited edition — the reason beyond the discount); the offer architecture (the discount is one lever — bundles, gift guides, and urgency of the real kind, shipping deadlines, carry the rest); the creative (native to each channel — the holiday ad that looks like a holiday ad gets skipped); the calendar (ship dates worked backward from the shipping deadlines — the only honest urgency in e-commerce). Rules: no fake urgency — urgency claims are sourced from real deadlines (shipping cutoffs), never invented (the countdown to nothing is detected and remembered); the discount has a floor (margin math done before the brief, not after); post-holiday retention planned (January buyers become February repeat buyers — or the campaign was a margin bonfire). Voice: direct — holiday campaigns fail from discount dependence; this one sells the story, prices honestly, and keeps the customer.""",
 output="""# Brief: Holiday Campaign\n**Outcome:** holiday revenue without margin destruction.\n**Angle:** the story beyond the discount (gift narrative, year-in-review, limited edition).\n**Offer architecture:** discount is one lever — bundles, gift guides, real shipping-deadline urgency carry the rest. No fake countdowns (detected and remembered).\n**Creative:** native per channel. **Calendar:** worked backward from ship dates.\n**Math first:** the discount floor set before the brief. **After:** January buyers → February repeats, or it was a margin bonfire.\nSell the story, price honestly, keep the customer.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
dict(family='cx-content-customer-story-arc', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['scientific-advertising#20'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Structure a customer story video.','audience':'Video team and customer.','constraints':'Customer approves the final cut.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — a story skeptics believe. Corpus: scientific-advertising#20 — traced returns: the story shows the before, the intervention, and the measured after. Arc: the before (the pain, in the customer's words — specific, dated); the search (what they tried that failed — the story earns credibility by admitting the alternatives); the intervention (what changed — specific enough to be useful); the after (the numbers — with the measurement caveats); the customer's verdict (their words, unscripted). Rules: the customer approves the final cut (their story, their sign-off — draft-first); no scripted testimonials (prompt the memory, not the lines); the failures stay in (a story without the failed attempts is an ad); numbers with bases and dates or they are cut. Voice: restrained — the story's power is its specificity; every generality removed in the edit. A customer story that could be about any product is about no product.""",
 output="""# Brief: Customer Story Video\n**Outcome:** a story skeptics believe.\n**Arc:** Before (pain, their words, dated) → the search (what failed first — credibility lives here) → intervention (specific enough to be useful) → after (numbers + caveats) → verdict (their words, unscripted).\n**Rules:** customer approves the final cut; prompt memories, never script lines; failures stay in (no failed attempts = an ad); numbers carry bases and dates or are cut.\nSpecificity is the power; every generality dies in the edit. A story that could be about any product is about no product.""",
 rules=['no-invented-proof','honest-labeling','propose-execute']),
dict(family='cx-content-smb-homepage-local', section='content-brief-agent',
 corpus_chunk_ids=['google-search-central#10'], license_class='TIER-2-OPEN',
 brief={'objective':'Brief a local service business homepage.','audience':'The owner-operator.','constraints':'Must win the local trust signals.'},
 reasoning_trace="""The role order for briefs: outcome first — the neighbor who calls. Corpus: google-search-central#10 — local trust signals: the real address, the real reviews, the real service area. Brief: hero — the service + the area ("Plumbing repair in [County]" — the searcher's words); trust row — years in business, review count with source, license number where applicable; the proof — before/after photos of real jobs (their work, not stock); the service area named (the towns, spelled out — "serving the greater [area]" is fog); the call CTA (the phone number, tap-to-call on mobile — the local conversion is a call, not a form); reviews linked, not just quoted (the skeptic checks). Every trust signal is the business's real data — years, review count with source, license number — labeled as verified facts, never stock claims. Rules: stock photos of handshakes banned (the owner in the truck beats the model with the wrench); every claim local and checkable. Voice: direct — local homepages fail from corporate cosplay; this one is a neighbor with proof. The site's job is the call; everything serves the call.""",
 output="""# Brief: Local Service Homepage\n**Outcome:** the neighbor who calls.\n**Hero:** service + area in the searcher's words. **Trust row:** years, review count + source, license number.\n**Proof:** before/after of real jobs (their work — stock handshakes banned). **Service area:** the towns, spelled out.\n**CTA:** the phone number, tap-to-call — the local conversion is a call.\n**Reviews:** linked, not just quoted (skeptics check).\nLocal pages fail from corporate cosplay. This one is a neighbor with proof — everything serves the call.""",
 rules=['honest-labeling','no-invented-proof']),
